<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pungnap Toseong</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pungnaptoseong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pungnap Toseong is a large earthen-walled site on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> in Songpa, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, built around the third century CE. Its walls, about 3.5 kilometres around, enclosed a royal centre, and many historians identify it as the northern fortress of Hanseong, the capital of Baekje until its fall to Goguryeo in 475. Excavations have found palace and ritual buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pungnap-toseong.jpg" alt="Pungnap Toseong" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:User:Straitgate · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pungnap_Toseong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
